# Act 1 — Arrival

## The Return

EXT. HOLLOW CREEK ROAD - DAY

A road that gave up being a road a mile back. MARCUS CADE walks it with a bag over one shoulder, coat too good for the country and too thin for the season.

He stops. Ahead, past the fence line, the farmhouse.

It is smaller than he has been remembering it. He stands with that a while.

Then he walks on.

INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - DAY (ARRIVAL)

ELEANOR CADE is at the sink with her back to the door. She does not turn when it opens.

ELEANOR
Shut it behind you.

Marcus sets the bag down. He leaves the door where it is.

MARCUS
It's warm out.

ELEANOR
(still not turning)
It isn't.

MARCUS
Is Ada's room still—

Eleanor turns the tap on. That is the whole answer.

She washes the same cup she has already washed. Behind her the door stands open on the yard, and neither of them closes it.

EXT. GRAVESIDE - DUSK

A short row of stones at the edge of the field. Eleanor stands at the newest one. REVEREND SHAW keeps a pace back with his hat in his hands.

SHAW
You don't have to come out here to see her.

ELEANOR
I know where she is. That's different.

Shaw waits to be asked for something. He is not asked.

# Act 2 — The Thaw

## The Thaw

EXT. CREEK CROSSING - DAY

The creek is running high and brown. Eleanor takes the crossing on foot and the current takes her legs from under her.

She goes down hard on her left side against the stones. Marcus is in the water before he has decided to be.

MARCUS
Hold on to me.

ELEANOR
(through her teeth)
I am.

He carries her up the bank. Neither of them looks back at the water.

INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT (AFTER)

Eleanor sits with her left arm held against her. Ada's shawl is folded over the back of the chair, where it has been folded for eleven years.

She puts it around her shoulders.

EXT. FARMHOUSE PORCH - DAY

## The Reckoning

INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT

One lamp. Eleanor at the table with Ada's shawl around her shoulders. Marcus in the doorway, coat still on.

ELEANOR
You came back.

MARCUS
I came back.

ELEANOR
Eleven years, and you come back for the part where there's nothing left to carry.

Marcus does not answer. His hand goes to his coat pocket and stops there.

ELEANOR
Say it or go to bed.

He goes to bed.

EXT. FARMHOUSE YARD - NIGHT (CUT)

Marcus crosses the yard to the truck and stands with his hand on the door. He does not open it.

INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT (LATER)

The lamp is out. Eleanor has not moved.

She sits in the dark with the shawl and the open door and the sound of the creek doing what it does.

# Act 3 — The Truth

INT. FARMHOUSE KITCHEN - NIGHT (CONFESSION)

The lamp again. Marcus has the locket out on the table between them, chain pooled beside it.

MARCUS
I was at the creek that day.

Eleanor looks at the locket. Not at him.

ELEANOR
I know.

MARCUS
Ada was already in the water.

ELEANOR
I know that too.

MARCUS
You know.

ELEANOR
I've known since the day after. I've been waiting eleven years for you to be the one to say it.

INT. HOLLOW CREEK CHURCH - DAY

Empty pews and one lit lamp. Eleanor sits near the back. Shaw does not ask her to come further forward.

SHAW
He's staying, then.

ELEANOR
He's stayed longer than I gave him.

## The Storm

EXT. RIDGE FOREST - NIGHT (STORM)

Rain coming sideways through the pines. Marcus goes up the ridge after the horses with a lamp that is no use to him.

Ahead of him, hoofprints filling with water.

## The Open Door

EXT. FARMHOUSE PORCH - DAWN

First light on the yard. The door stands open, as it has stood since he came.

Eleanor is on the porch when Marcus comes up from the barn. She holds on to him. It takes him a moment to understand that is what is happening.
